#!/usr/bin/env bash
# restore: put the plugin's data back from a dot-backup snapshot. (optional)
#
# Run by dot-restore, after your dotfiles are restored, for every plugin that stored data in the
# chosen snapshot AND is installed now. So restoring an old snapshot never triggers a plugin
# that didn't exist then, and on a new machine you install plugins first (dot-init), then run
# dot-restore: it restores the plugin data even when your files already match the backup.
# Read what your `backup` hook wrote from $DOT_BACKUP_SRC and put it where it belongs.
#
# Contract
#   Exit 0      success.
#   Exit != 0   failure: reported, and dot-restore carries on. Run dot-restore again after
#               fixing it.
#   Be rerunnable: it may run again for the same snapshot.
#   Don't overwrite files silently if they may hold newer changes: dot-restore saves a
#   "pre-restore" snapshot (including this plugin's `backup` data) first, but say what you did.
#   Same rules as `init`: non-interactive, output only on failure, no time limit, `needs-sudo`.
#
# Environment
#   DOT_PLUGIN, DOT_PLUGIN_DIR, DOT_OS, DOT_ROOT, DOT_COMMON: as in `init`.
#   DOT_HOOK        "restore"
#   DOT_BACKUP_SRC  this plugin's folder inside the snapshot, exactly as `backup` left it
#                   (read-only: don't modify the snapshot)
#
# Arguments: none.
#
# Example:
#   xargs -n1 code --install-extension < "$DOT_BACKUP_SRC/extensions.txt"
